{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050884 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204089 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203852 :question/math-academy-id "e-17087" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\nI. $4z + 3 = 4z + 9$\n\nII. $2z - 5 = z + 1$\n\nIII. $3z - 2 = 3z - 2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4z+3 & =4z+9 \\\\ 4z+3-4z & =4z+9-4z \\\\ 3 & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $z.$ Therefore, the equation $4z + 3 = 4z + 9$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z-5 & =z+1 \\\\ 2z-5-z+5 & =z+1-z+5 \\\\ z & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2z - 5 = z + 1$ has the unique solution $z = 6.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z-2 & =3z-2 \\\\ 3z-2-3z & =3z-2-3z \\\\ -2 & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204600 :question/math-academy-id "q-287964" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $2x = x + 1$\n- **II.** $2x + 1 = 2x + 1$\n- **III.** $2x + 1 = 2x + 3$" :question/worked-solution "The equations reduce respectively to x=1, an identity, and 1=3. Only the third has no solutions."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204601 :question/math-academy-id "q-288058" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $4x - 2 = 4x + 3$\n- **II.** $3x + 4 = 3x + 4$\n- **III.** $5x = 2x + 6$" :question/worked-solution "The equations reduce respectively to -2=3, an identity, and x=2. Only the first has no solutions."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297172 :question/math-academy-id "q-288066" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $-3u+4=-3u-1$\n- **II.** $2u+5=2u+8$\n- **III.** $6u-2=3u+1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3u+4 & =-3u-1 \\\\ -3u+4+3u & =-3u-1+3u \\\\ 4 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $u.$ Therefore, the equation $-3u+4=-3u-1$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2u+5 & =2u+8 \\\\ 2u+5-2u & =2u+8-2u \\\\ 5 & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $u.$ Therefore, the equation $2u+5=2u+8$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6u-2 & =3u+1 \\\\ 6u-2-3u+2 & =3u+1-3u+2 \\\\ 3u & =3 \\\\ u & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $6u-2=3u+1$ has the unique solution $u=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297173 :question/math-academy-id "q-288070" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $-4y+2=-4y-5$\n- **II.** $6y+3=6y-1$\n- **III.** $5y-7=2y+1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+2 & =-4y-5 \\\\ -4y+2+4y & =-4y-5+4y \\\\ 2 & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $y.$ Therefore, the equation $-4y+2=-4y-5$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6y+3 & =6y-1 \\\\ 6y+3-6y & =6y-1-6y \\\\ 3 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $y.$ Therefore, the equation $6y+3=6y-1$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y-7 & =2y+1 \\\\ 5y-7-2y+7 & =2y+1-2y+7 \\\\ 3y & =8 \\\\ y & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $5y-7=2y+1$ has the unique solution $y=\\frac{8}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297174 :question/math-academy-id "q-288072" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $2z-4=2z+1$\n- **II.** $5z+6=5z+6$\n- **III.** $3z+2=z+4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z-4 & =2z+1 \\\\ 2z-4-2z & =2z+1-2z \\\\ -4 & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $z.$ Therefore, the equation $2z-4=2z+1$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5z+6 & =5z+6 \\\\ 5z+6-5z & =5z+6-5z \\\\ 6 & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $z.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z+2 & =z+4 \\\\ 3z+2-z-2 & =z+4-z-2 \\\\ 2z & =2 \\\\ z & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $3z+2=z+4$ has the unique solution $z=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297175 :question/math-academy-id "q-288073" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $3w+2=2w+2$\n- **II.** $4w-7=4w-7$\n- **III.** $6w+5=6w+9$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3w+2 & =2w+2 \\\\ 3w+2-2w-2 & =2w+2-2w-2 \\\\ w & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $3w+2=2w+2$ has the unique solution $w=0.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4w-7 & =4w-7 \\\\ 4w-7-4w & =4w-7-4w \\\\ -7 & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $w.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6w+5 & =6w+9 \\\\ 6w+5-6w & =6w+9-6w \\\\ 5 & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $w.$ Therefore, the equation $6w+5=6w+9$ has no solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297176 :question/math-academy-id "q-288074" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $9v-5=4v+2$\n- **II.** $2v+3=2v+7$\n- **III.** $5v-1=5v-1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9v-5 & =4v+2 \\\\ 9v-5-4v+5 & =4v+2-4v+5 \\\\ 5v & =7 \\\\ v & =\\frac{7}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $9v-5=4v+2$ has the unique solution $v=\\frac{7}{5}.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2v+3 & =2v+7 \\\\ 2v+3-2v & =2v+7-2v \\\\ 3 & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $v.$ Therefore, the equation $2v+3=2v+7$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5v-1 & =5v-1 \\\\ 5v-1-5v & =5v-1-5v \\\\ -1 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $v.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508100 :question/math-academy-id "q-288069" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\nI. $2x-3=2x+4$\n\nII. $-5x+7=-2x-2$\n\nIII. $4x+1=4x+1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3 & =2x+4 \\\\ 2x-3-2x & =2x+4-2x \\\\ -3 & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $2x-3=2x+4$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5x+7 & =-2x-2 \\\\ -5x+7+2x-7 & =-2x-2+2x-7 \\\\ -3x & =-9 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $-5x+7=-2x-2$ has the unique solution $x=3.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+1 & =4x+1 \\\\ 4x+1-4x & =4x+1-4x \\\\ 1 & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation With No Solutions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204090 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203853 :question/math-academy-id "e-17213" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $y,$ given by\n\n$$\n4(r - 2)y = -8\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\neq 0.$ If $a = 0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ay = b.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na = 4(r - 2), \\qquad b = -8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n4(r - 2) = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(r-2) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4(r-2)}{4} & =\\frac{0}{4} \\\\ r-2 & =0 \\\\ r & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204596 :question/math-academy-id "q-287665" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n2px=3\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=b.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=2p,\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2p=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204602 :question/math-academy-id "q-288593" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $y,$ given by\n\n$$\n(q-1)y=4\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $q?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ay=b.$ In this case,\n\n$$\na=q-1,\\,b=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nq-1=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q-1 & =0 \\\\ q & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297177 :question/math-academy-id "q-288135" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $y,$ given by\n\n$$\n4py=-2\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ay=b.$ In this case,\n\n$$\na=4p,\\,b=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n4p=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297178 :question/math-academy-id "q-288143" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n-3(q+4)x=6\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $q?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=b.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=-3(q+4),\\,b=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n-3(q+4)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3(q+4) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{-3(q+4)}{-3} & =\\frac{0}{-3} \\\\ q+4 & =0 \\\\ q & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297179 :question/math-academy-id "q-288144" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $z,$ given by\n\n$$\n-5(p-9)z=10\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $az=b.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=-5(p-9),\\,b=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n-5(p-9)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5(p-9) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{-5(p-9)}{-5} & =\\frac{0}{-5} \\\\ p-9 & =0 \\\\ p & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297197 :question/math-academy-id "q-288596" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $y,$ given by\n\n$$\n(q+9)y=-6\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $q?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ay=b.$ In this case,\n\n$$\na=q+9,\\,b=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nq+9=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q+9 & =0 \\\\ q & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508071 :question/math-academy-id "q-288107" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $t,$ given by\n\n$$\n5qt=-4\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $q?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $at=b.$ In this case,\n\n$$\na=5q,\\,b=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n5q=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\nq=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570777 :question/math-academy-id "q-287666" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n2(p-1)x=5\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=b.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=2(p-1),\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2(p-1)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(p-1) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2(p-1)}{2} & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\ p-1 & =0 \\\\ p & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204091 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203878 :question/math-academy-id "e-18853" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $z,$ given by\n\n$$\n(r - 9)z + 2 = -1\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\neq 0.$ If $a = 0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $az = b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-9)z+2 & =-1 \\\\ (r-9)z+2-2 & =-1-2 \\\\ \\underbrace{(r-9)}_{a}z & =\\underbrace{-3}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nr - 9 = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r-9 & =0 \\\\ r & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204597 :question/math-academy-id "q-287667" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n2(p-4)x+2=5\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(p-4)x+2 & =5 \\\\ 2(p-4)x+2-2 & =5-2 \\\\ \\underbrace{2(p-4)}_{a}x & =\\underbrace{3}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2(p-4)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(p-4) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2(p-4)}{2} & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\ p-4 & =0 \\\\ p & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204603 :question/math-academy-id "q-288643" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $z$, given by\n$(r + 3)z + 5 =-4$\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $r$?$r =$\n{{field-1}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297201 :question/math-academy-id "q-288634" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n3(p+2)x+7=10\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(p+2)x+7 & =10 \\\\ 3(p+2)x+7-7 & =10-7 \\\\ \\underbrace{3(p+2)}_{a}x & =\\underbrace{3}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n3(p+2)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(p+2) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3(p+2)}{3} & =\\frac{0}{3} \\\\ p+2 & =0 \\\\ p & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297202 :question/math-academy-id "q-288644" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $v,$ given by\n\n$$\n(k-2)v-3=5\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $av=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k-2)v-3 & =5 \\\\ (k-2)v-3+3 & =5+3 \\\\ \\underbrace{(k-2)}_{a}v & =\\underbrace{8}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nk-2=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k-2 & =0 \\\\ k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297203 :question/math-academy-id "q-288646" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n(m+6)x+2=7\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $m?$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(m+6)x+2 & =7 \\\\ (m+6)x+2-2 & =7-2 \\\\ \\underbrace{(m+6)}_{a}x & =\\underbrace{5}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nm+6=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m+6 & =0 \\\\ m & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297204 :question/math-academy-id "q-288648" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $u,$ given by\n\n$$\n(q-5)u+9=0\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $q?$\n\n$q=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $au=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(q-5)u+9 & =0 \\\\ (q-5)u+9-9 & =0-9 \\\\ \\underbrace{(q-5)}_{a}u & =\\underbrace{-9}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nq-5=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q-5 & =0 \\\\ q & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477030 :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $z,$ given by\n\n$$\n(p+2)z-8=-3\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $az=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p+2)z-8 & =-3 \\\\ (p+2)z-8+8 & =-3+8 \\\\ \\underbrace{(p+2)}_{a}z & =\\underbrace{5}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\np+2=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p+2 & =0 \\\\ p & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477031 :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $w,$ given by\n\n$$\n5(r-5)w-6=4\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $r?$\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $aw=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(r-5)w-6 & =4 \\\\ 5(r-5)w-6+6 & =4+6 \\\\ \\underbrace{5(r-5)}_{a}w & =\\underbrace{10}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n5(r-5)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(r-5) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{5(r-5)}{5} & =\\frac{0}{5} \\\\ r-5 & =0 \\\\ r & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477034 :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $v,$ given by\n\n$$\n2(q+5)v+6=-4\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $q?$\n\n$q=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $av=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(q+5)v+6 & =-4 \\\\ 2(q+5)v+6-6 & =-4-6 \\\\ \\underbrace{2(q+5)}_{a}v & =\\underbrace{-10}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2(q+5)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(q+5) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2(q+5)}{2} & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\ q+5 & =0 \\\\ q & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477035 :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $z,$ given by\n\n$$\n7(p-3)z-8=2\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $az=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(p-3)z-8 & =2 \\\\ 7(p-3)z-8+8 & =2+8 \\\\ \\underbrace{7(p-3)}_{a}z & =\\underbrace{10}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n7(p-3)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(p-3) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{7(p-3)}{7} & =\\frac{0}{7} \\\\ p-3 & =0 \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477039 :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $w,$ given by\n\n$$\n(r-3)w-6=-5\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $r?$\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $aw=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-3)w-6 & =-5 \\\\ (r-3)w-6+6 & =-5+6 \\\\ \\underbrace{(r-3)}_{a}w & =\\underbrace{1}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nr-3=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r-3 & =0 \\\\ r & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508045 :question/math-academy-id "q-288639" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $v,$ given by\n\n$$\n2(q-7)v+5=1\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $q?$\n\n$q=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $av=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(q-7)v+5 & =1 \\\\ 2(q-7)v+5-5 & =1-5 \\\\ \\underbrace{2(q-7)}_{a}v & =\\underbrace{-4}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2(q-7)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(q-7) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2(q-7)}{2} & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\ q-7 & =0 \\\\ q & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508108 :question/math-academy-id "q-288637" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $z,$ given by\n\n$$\n-7(k+3)z+8=1\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $az=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7(k+3)z+8 & =1 \\\\ -7(k+3)z+8-8 & =1-8 \\\\ \\underbrace{-7(k+3)}_{a}z & =\\underbrace{-7}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n-7(k+3)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7(k+3) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{-7(k+3)}{-7} & =\\frac{0}{-7} \\\\ k+3 & =0 \\\\ k & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter - Constants on Both Sides"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204092 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203854 :question/math-academy-id "e-17214" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n2nx + 7  = -4x + 5\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $n?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\neq 0.$ If $a = 0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $ax = b.$ \n\nWe start by adding $4x$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2nx+7 & =-4x+5 \\\\ 2nx+4x+7 & =-4x+5+4x \\\\ 2nx+4x+7 & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $7$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2nx+4x+7 & =5 \\\\ 2nx+4x+7-7 & =5-7 \\\\ 2nx+4x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $x$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(2n + 4)x  = -2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ax = b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(2n + 4)}_{a}x  = \\underbrace{-2}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2n + 4= 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2n+4 & =0 \\\\ 2n+4-4 & =0-4 \\\\ 2n & =-4 \\\\ \\frac{2n}{2} & =\\frac{-4}{2} \\\\ n & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204598 :question/math-academy-id "q-287677" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n2px=3x+5\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $ax=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $3x$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2px & =3x+5 \\\\ 2px-3x & =3x+5-3x \\\\ 2px-3x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $x$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(2p-3)x=5.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(2p-3)}_{a}x=\\underbrace{5}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2p-3=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2p-3 & =0 \\\\ 2p-3+3 & =0+3 \\\\ 2p & =3 \\\\ \\frac{2p}{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204599 :question/math-academy-id "q-287678" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $x$, given by\n$2px + 6 = 4x + 5$\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p$?$p =$\n{{field-1}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297205 :question/math-academy-id "q-288662" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $y,$ given by\n\n$$\n3py=5y+8\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $ay=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $5y$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3py & =5y+8 \\\\ 3py-5y & =5y+8-5y \\\\ 3py-5y & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $y$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(3p-5)y=8.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ay=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(3p-5)}_{a}y=\\underbrace{8}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n3p-5=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p-5 & =0 \\\\ 3p-5+5 & =0+5 \\\\ 3p & =5 \\\\ \\frac{3p}{3} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ p & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297206 :question/math-academy-id "q-288673" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $z,$ given by\n\n$$\n3qz+4=7z+1\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $q?$\n\n$q=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $az=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $7z$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3qz+4 & =7z+1 \\\\ 3qz-7z+4 & =7z+1-7z \\\\ 3qz-7z+4 & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $4$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3qz-7z+4 & =1 \\\\ 3qz-7z+4-4 & =1-4 \\\\ 3qz-7z & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $z$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(3q-7)z=-3.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $az=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(3q-7)}_{a}z=\\underbrace{-3}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n3q-7=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3q-7 & =0 \\\\ 3q-7+7 & =0+7 \\\\ 3q & =7 \\\\ \\frac{3q}{3} & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ q & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297207 :question/math-academy-id "q-288674" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $u,$ given by\n\n$$\n5ru+3=2u+6\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $r?$\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $au=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $2u$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5ru+3 & =2u+6 \\\\ 5ru-2u+3 & =2u+6-2u \\\\ 5ru-2u+3 & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $3$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5ru-2u+3 & =6 \\\\ 5ru-2u+3-3 & =6-3 \\\\ 5ru-2u & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $u$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(5r-2)u=3.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $au=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(5r-2)}_{a}u=\\underbrace{3}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n5r-2=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5r-2 & =0 \\\\ 5r-2+2 & =0+2 \\\\ 5r & =2 \\\\ \\frac{5r}{5} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297208 :question/math-academy-id "q-288675" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $w,$ given by\n\n$$\n-4mw+5=-3w+1\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $m?$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $aw=b.$ \n\nWe start by adding $3w$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4mw+5 & =-3w+1 \\\\ -4mw+3w+5 & =-3w+1+3w \\\\ -4mw+3w+5 & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $5$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4mw+3w+5 & =1 \\\\ -4mw+3w+5-5 & =1-5 \\\\ -4mw+3w & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $w$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(-4m+3)w=-4.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $aw=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(-4m+3)}_{a}w=\\underbrace{-4}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n-4m+3=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4m+3 & =0 \\\\ -4m+3-3 & =0-3 \\\\ -4m & =-3 \\\\ \\frac{-4m}{-4} & =\\frac{-3}{-4} \\\\ m & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297209 :question/math-academy-id "q-288678" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $z,$ given by\n\n$$\n-3pz-8=-z-6\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $az=b.$ \n\nWe start by adding $z$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3pz-8 & =-z-6 \\\\ -3pz+z-8 & =-z-6+z \\\\ -3pz+z-8 & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we add $8$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3pz+z-8 & =-6 \\\\ -3pz+z-8+8 & =-6+8 \\\\ -3pz+z & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $z$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(-3p+1)z=2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(-3p+1)}_{a}z=\\underbrace{2}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n-3p+1=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3p+1 & =0 \\\\ -3p+1-1 & =0-1 \\\\ -3p & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{-3p}{-3} & =\\frac{-1}{-3} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477046 :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $v,$ given by\n\n$$\n3nv=6v+6\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $n?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $av=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $6v$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3nv & =6v+6 \\\\ 3nv-6v & =6v+6-6v \\\\ 3nv-6v & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $v$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(3n-6)v=6.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $av=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(3n-6)}_{a}v=\\underbrace{6}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n3n-6=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n-6 & =0 \\\\ 3n-6+6 & =0+6 \\\\ 3n & =6 \\\\ \\frac{3n}{3} & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\ n & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477050 :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $w,$ given by\n\n$$\n5nw=8w+9\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $n?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $aw=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $8w$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5nw & =8w+9 \\\\ 5nw-8w & =8w+9-8w \\\\ 5nw-8w & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $w$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(5n-8)w=9.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $aw=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(5n-8)}_{a}w=\\underbrace{9}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n5n-8=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5n-8 & =0 \\\\ 5n-8+8 & =0+8 \\\\ 5n & =8 \\\\ \\frac{5n}{5} & =\\frac{8}{5} \\\\ n & =\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477060 :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $w,$ given by\n\n$$\n2kw=8w+6\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $aw=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $8w$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2kw & =8w+6 \\\\ 2kw-8w & =8w+6-8w \\\\ 2kw-8w & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $w$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(2k-8)w=6.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $aw=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(2k-8)}_{a}w=\\underbrace{6}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2k-8=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k-8 & =0 \\\\ 2k-8+8 & =0+8 \\\\ 2k & =8 \\\\ \\frac{2k}{2} & =\\frac{8}{2} \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477062 :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n7px=5x+9\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $ax=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $5x$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7px & =5x+9 \\\\ 7px-5x & =5x+9-5x \\\\ 7px-5x & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $x$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(7p-5)x=9.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(7p-5)}_{a}x=\\underbrace{9}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n7p-5=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7p-5 & =0 \\\\ 7p-5+5 & =0+5 \\\\ 7p & =5 \\\\ \\frac{7p}{7} & =\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ p & =\\frac{5}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477066 :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $u,$ given by\n\n$$\n4qu+4=9u-8\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $q?$\n\n$q=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $au=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $9u$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4qu+4 & =9u-8 \\\\ 4qu-9u+4 & =9u-8-9u \\\\ 4qu-9u+4 & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $4$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4qu-9u+4 & =-8 \\\\ 4qu-9u+4-4 & =-8-4 \\\\ 4qu-9u & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $u$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(4q-9)u=-12.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $au=b:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(4q-9)}_{a}u=\\underbrace{-12}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n4q-9=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4q-9 & =0 \\\\ 4q-9+9 & =0+9 \\\\ 4q & =9 \\\\ \\frac{4q}{4} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ q & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter - Variables and Constants on Both Sides"}] :topic/title "Linear Equations With No Solutions"}
